msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:631
msgid ""
"Two different failures: a record pointing at a deleted file, and a file "
"whose stored hash no longer matches what is on disk. The second one is the "
"serious one — it means the certification is lying."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:636
msgid "Monthly, and after any migration of the media folder."
msgstr "Cada mes, y después de cualquier migración de la carpeta de media."

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:643
msgid "Cache"
msgstr "Cache"

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:645
msgid "Checks that the cache answers and that it is shared across processes."
msgstr "Comprueba que la cache responde y que se comparte entre procesos."

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:649
msgid ""
"Asking the VPS whether Redis is alive does not answer what matters: whether "
"this application reaches it, and whether the rate counters are shared. And "
"it cannot be judged by eye, because django-redis runs with IGNORE_EXCEPTIONS "
"so an outage does not take the login down — the price is that an unreachable "
"server returns None instead of raising, and a broken cache looks exactly "
"like an empty one. The last check is the one that decides: it writes a key "
"and reads it back from another process."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:658
msgid ""
"After pointing REDIS_URL at the VPS, and when rate limits seem not to apply."
msgstr ""
"Después de apuntar REDIS_URL al VPS, y cuando parezca que los límites de "
"tasa no se aplican."

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:666
msgid "Background workers"
msgstr "Workers en segundo plano"

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:668
msgid ""
"Checks whether this server can hold a worker process outside the request."
msgstr ""
"Comprueba si este servidor puede sostener un proceso worker fuera de la "
"petición."

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:672
msgid ""
"A worker is not a library you install: it is a process that lives outside "
"the request and does not die. Shared hosting often kills anything that is "
"not serving a page, and never says so, so this is not decided by reading "
"documentation — it is measured. It also checks something easy to miss: Redis "
"working as a cache does not mean it works as a broker, because the ACL is "
"written for the cache and a broker needs other keys and other commands. That "
"one fails silently, with tasks simply vanishing."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:681
msgid "Before deciding whether Celery is worth adopting here."
msgstr "Antes de decidir si merece la pena adoptar Celery aquí."

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:688
msgid "Launch the test process"
msgstr "Lanzar el proceso de prueba"

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:691
msgid ""
"Starts a detached process that does nothing but write the time every 30 "
"seconds. Come back later and run this again: what matters is not that it "
"starts, but that it is still alive then."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:699
msgid "How long the test should last"
msgstr "Cuánto debe durar la prueba"

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:703
msgid ""
"Half an hour rules out the hosting that kills everything right away, which "
"is the common case. It says nothing about the one that kills a process now "
"and then, and for that it has to run longer: 1440 is a full day."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:715
msgid ""
"Checks whether the scheduled tasks are actually installed in the crontab."
msgstr ""
"Comprueba si las tareas programadas están instaladas de verdad en el crontab."

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:719
msgid ""
"Declaring a task in CRONJOBS does not schedule it: django-crontab writes it "
"into the crontab only when someone runs \"manage.py crontab add\". Until "
"then the list exists in the code and nobody runs it — with no error and "
"nothing in the log. There is a worse case too: the installed line carries a "
"hash of the whole job definition, schedule included, so changing a schedule "
"leaves the line pointing at a job that no longer exists. The cron fires and "
"runs nothing, and from outside it looks exactly like everything working."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:729
#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:1155
msgid "When something that should run on its own has not run."
msgstr "Cuando algo que debería correr solo no ha corrido."

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:736
msgid "Mature blockchain proofs"
msgstr "Madurar pruebas de la cadena de bloques"

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:738
msgid ""
"Asks the calendars whether the pending proofs are already in a Bitcoin block."
msgstr ""
"Pregunta a los calendarios si las pruebas pendientes ya están en un bloque "
"de Bitcoin."

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:742
msgid ""
"This is what the scheduled task does every 15 minutes. Running it by hand "
"catches up without waiting — useful when the task was not installed, or "
"right after installing it. It is safe to run at any time: with nothing "
"pending it does not even reach the network, and a proof that has not matured "
"yet is left for the next round rather than treated as an error."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:749
msgid "When an anchor has been waiting longer than a few hours."
msgstr "Cuando un anclaje lleva esperando más de unas horas."

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:756
msgid "Dependencies"
msgstr "Dependencias"

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:758
msgid ""
"Checks that requirements.txt carries everything declared in pyproject.toml."
msgstr ""
"Comprueba que requirements.txt lleve todo lo declarado en pyproject.toml."

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:762
msgid ""
"The project uses two dependency files: pyproject.toml with uv locally, and "
"requirements.txt with pip in production, because cPanel has no uv. The "
"bridge between them is a manual export, and a manual step is a step that "
"gets forgotten. It already happened: opentimestamps was declared a day "
"before it reached requirements.txt, and in between, production had no "
"library and blockchain anchoring failed. The symptom showed up far from the "
"cause, which is the worst kind."
msgstr ""

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:771
msgid "Before every deploy, and after adding any dependency."
msgstr "Antes de cada despliegue, y después de añadir cualquier dependencia."

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:778
msgid "Blockchain anchoring"
msgstr "Anclaje en la cadena de bloques"

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:780
msgid ""
"Checks whether this server can send hashes to the OpenTimestamps calendars."
msgstr ""
"Comprueba si este servidor puede enviar huellas a los calendarios de "
"OpenTimestamps."

#: .\apps\project\specific\internal\ops\registry.py:784
msgid ""
"When the composer says the box was sealed but not sent, the cause is almost "
"never the code: either the opentimestamps library is not installed on this "
"server, or the hosting blocks outbound connections. This tells the two "
"apart, which is what decides whether the fix is a pip install of "
"requirements.txt or a call to the provider. It also reports how the real "
"anchors are doing: a proof that has been waiting for days means nothing is "
